       }
      ],
      "text": "Bilateral hyperplasia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " surgery not recommended (70% stay hypertensive after bilateral adrenalectomy); usually controlled by an aldosterone blocker such as spironolactone, or amiloride, or another K-sparing diuretic."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Spironolactone 50 mg PO daily, increased over 1–3 mo to usually 50–100 mg daily; amiloride 5–10 mg PO daily; eplerenone 50 mg PO daily up to 200 mg BID."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Spironolactone blocks the androgen receptor (gynecomastia, sexual dysfunction); eplerenone doesn't, so it's the drug of choice for long-term tx in males unless low-dose spironolactone works."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
